<!-- ===== SECTION 6: MILESTONES ===== -->
<section class="section" id="milestones">
  <div class="section-label">Section 05</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Beyond aggregate statistics, individual moments and records animate the history of the category.
  </p>

  <div class="milestone-grid">
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">Category Origin</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">First-Ever Winner: Louis Silvers, 1934</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">One Night of Love claimed the inaugural Best Score award at the 6th Academy Awards, establishing the category that would run — with periodic restructuring — for nine decades and counting.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">Longest Win Gap</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Hans Zimmer: 27 Years Between Wins</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">Zimmer won for The Lion King (1994) and then waited 27 years before Dune (2021) brought his second Oscar — the longest gap between wins in the dataset.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">First Back-to-Back</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Franz Waxman: 1950–1951</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">Sunset Boulevard (1950) followed immediately by A Place in the Sun (1951) — the first back-to-back wins for a single composer in the category's history.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">Animated Film Dominance</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Alan Menken's 3-Year Streak, 1989–1992</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">The Little Mermaid, Beauty and the Beast, and Aladdin — three consecutive wins for Menken during Disney's Renaissance, the most compressed multi-win streak of the modern era.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">All-Time Speed Record</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Ludwig Göransson: 4 Wins in 8 Years</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">From Black Panther (2018) to Sinners (2026), Göransson accumulated 4 wins faster than any composer in Oscar history. The three-way tie at the summit (with Barry and Williams) makes him a co-record holder at just 40 years old.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">Global Diversity Breakthrough</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">A.R. Rahman — First Indian Winner, 2008</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">Slumdog Millionaire's score signaled the Academy's expanding geographic scope. That same year represented a broader shift toward non-Western composers that defines the contemporary era.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">Largest Ensemble Win</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Stagecoach (1939): Four Composers Shared One Oscar</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">Richard Hageman, W. Franke Harling, John Leipold, and Leo Shuken shared the award for Stagecoach — the largest credited ensemble in the category's history.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">Most Recent Winner</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Ludwig Göransson — Sinners, 2026</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">The 98th Academy Awards ceremony in March 2026 awarded Göransson his fourth Oscar, for Ryan Coogler's Sinners. Per dataset documentation, this was described as his "third historic win" — the discrepancy may reflect how Oppenheimer's two-year listing is counted in different contexts.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="milestone-badge">First Woman Winner</div>
      <div class="milestone-title">Hildur Guðnadóttir — Joker, 2019</div>
      <div class="milestone-detail">Guðnadóttir became the first woman to win Best Original Score as a solo composer, marking a historic gender barrier broken 85 years after the category was established.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION 7: CONCLUSIONS ===== -->
<section class="section" id="conclusions">
  <div class="section-label">Section 06</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Five data-grounded observations about where the Oscar scoring category has been and where it appears to be heading.
  </p>
  <ul class="takeaway-list">
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">01</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>A new record-holder has arrived, and he is remarkably young.</strong> Ludwig Göransson's 4 wins in 8 years (2018–2026) tie him with John Barry (who took 24 years) and John Williams (18 years) at the top of the all-time leaderboard. The compression of Göransson's achievement suggests either an unusually dominant stylistic moment or a structural shift in which kinds of films — prestige genre, blockbuster auteur — are now favored by Oscar voters.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">02</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>The contemporary era is simultaneously more diverse and more concentrated than any previous period.</strong> With 21 unique winners across 27 ceremonies, the 2000–2026 era has the most geographic and stylistic breadth. Yet its repeat-win rate (37%) is the highest of any era — winners like Göransson, Shore, Desplat, Santaolalla, and Reznor/Ross cluster in ways that suggest a two-tier market: a handful of globally-branded composers competing for prestige blockbusters, with one-time winners filling the remaining slots.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">03</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>The 1980s remain the only decade to achieve maximum compositional diversity.</strong> Ten unique winners in ten ceremonies — every single year brought a new face. This anomaly coincides with the collapse of the old studio system composer hierarchy and predates the rise of today's globe-spanning A-list. It may be the only decade in the category's history where genuinely no one was the frontrunner going into any given year.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">04</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>Three eras, three defining archetypes of dominance:</strong> the Golden Age produced institutional dominance (staff composers winning by volume), the Silver-to-Modern transition produced career dominance (Barry, Williams, Jarre winning for iconic "big" films over decades), and the contemporary era is producing brand dominance (one composer's aesthetic becoming synonymous with a particular kind of prestigious film). Each shift tracks broader changes in how Hollywood finances and markets film music.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-num">05</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>Gender and geographic diversity accelerated sharply post-2000 but remain incomplete.</strong> Of the 69 unique winning entries, only one — Hildur Guðnadóttir (2019) — is a solo woman winner. The contemporary era brought composers from India, Iceland, Argentina, Germany, Canada, and beyond, but the data shows this openness coexists with, rather than displacing, a small dominant tier. If Göransson's pace continues, the late 2020s could see the first composer to break the 4-win ceiling since the category was established in 1934.
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

